On discovery, Grandad promptly burned the book and rewarded little Jamie with his first smart tablet.\
\'93I want a definition of 
\f2\i libertarian 
\f0\i0 by breakfast,\'94 Grandad had said. \'93And I expect the word 
\f2\i tax
\f0\i0  to be in it.\'94\
Little Jamie laughed. \'93That\'92s a swear word,\'94 he said.\
\'93Yes it is,\'94 Grandad agreed. Little Jamie was allowed to say 
\f2\i fuck
\f0\i0  and 
\f2\i cunt
\f0\i0  and 
\f2\i audit
\f0\i0  before he was allowed to say 
\f2\i tax
\f0\i0 .\
Jamison Hardy shook the reverie out of his head. The meeting had moved on\'97protection of culturally significant landmarks was assured. A government minister had been targeted and persuaded, and Jamison Hardy\'92s beachfront mansion would be renovated at taxpayer\'92s expense. Plus he\'92d get a new swimming pool, and an increased security budget. Just to tax with the Minister of Defence\'97whom he despised\'97Jamison Hardy wanted a tank. He got two.\
Then a lab coat was shown in. Despite the young man wearing a good suit that his mother had probably chosen, he was still a lab coat in Hardy\'92s estimation. He wore a HIBReLabs badge pinned to his suit that identified him as a doctor. Hardy never read the lab coat\'92s name, and sighed when the lab coat took the kind of deep breath that preceded a rehearsed speech. Mercifully, he was working from what appeared to be a two-page summary; but he got through less than eleven words of it before the mood changed, and the room started interrupting him with congratulations.\

\f2\i Every disease! Amazing!
\f0\i0 \
Not the 
\f2\i treatment
\f0\i0  of every disease, not the 
\f2\i management
\f0\i0  of them; the 
\f2\i end
\f0\i0  of them.\
Some in the room thought of their cancer-riddled mothers, others their stubborn cases of foot fungus. The lab coat spoke of bones that mended, organs that repaired and regenerated, a lifespan nobody at the labs had yet been game to put a number on. The young man said the words \'93no longer necessary\'94 three times about three different industries.\
The seats at the table were delighted. Two applauded. One said it was the finest thing the company had ever done.\
Hardy\'92s attention piqued; there was something odd about the way that seat praised the work. It took him a good few seconds to twig why it seemed so out of place. The seat was being 
\f2\i sincere
\f0\i0 . They all were. The mood was completely absent of turf protection and ladder climbing. None of them had their political theodolites out to survey all the angles.\
Hardy went quiet. He\'92d been doing financial arithmetic since Grandad gave him that first tablet. Hardy owned hospitals, and he was proud of the way he\'92d been able to pare the costs of treatment down while churning patients through faster than at any other time in history. He owned insurers who paid hospitals, medical funds that owned the insurers, a respectable slice of the pharmaceutical concerns that sold to both, and the debt of the people who could not afford any of it.\
\'93You\'92re all fired,\'94 Hardy stated with measured ice. \'93Get out.\'94\
The lab coat, wide-eyed behind his glasses, turned a finger against his chest. 
\f2\i Me too?
\f0\i0 \
Hardy rose and thanked the lab coat warmly, and then, because it paid to be courteous, he thanked him again. Thanking was still one of the best ways to soften up an adversary before he put them in one of his fast-track casualty wards.\
The lab coat glowed all the way to the lift, believing\'97despite the wholesale sackings, which after all didn\'92t include him\'97he had been heard and believed and, perhaps, even trusted.
